  :adr/body
  "# ADR-2607197300: Concurrent PostgreSQL pool scheduling

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-19

## Decision

Each physical pool connection owns an independent compiled Kotoba database
provider instance, Wasm scratch page and execution lock. The native metadata
monitor protects only pools, leases, the FIFO waiter queue and scratch
ownership; it is not held while a query or reset executes. This is required
because a Chicory Wasm instance has an interpreter stack that cannot safely
execute concurrent exports, while separate compiled provider instances can run
in parallel without moving PostgreSQL protocol logic into the JVM boundary.

Saturated acquisitions join a host-owned FIFO queue. Only its head may claim a
released or newly grown connection, and every waiter retains the fixed host
deadline. Release removes the opaque lease before waiting for the connection
execution lock. A query which already passed lease validation completes before
reset; later calls with that token fail closed.

## Evidence

Released PostgreSQL 14.19 runs two `pg_sleep(0.4)` queries through distinct
guest consumers and physical connections below a 650 ms upper bound. Two
queued consumers acquire in FIFO order. A separate 0.6 second query/release
race proves release waits for the in-flight operation, resets successfully and
then rejects the stale lease. All physical handles close.

This closes true per-connection query concurrency, FIFO waiter scheduling and
query/release race safety. Abrupt database restart, network fault recovery,
version/platform matrices and long-duration soak remain open."}]
